      "text": "Note that \\[\\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{\\frac{x}{y}}{\\frac{x}{y} + 1}.\\]Therefore,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} &= \\frac{(x + y)(x + y)}{x^2 + y^2} \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{x}{y} + 1 \\right) \\frac{\\left( \\frac{x}{y} + 1 \\right)}{\\frac{x}{y} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{y} + 1 + \\frac{y}{x} + \\frac{1}{\\frac{x}{y} + 1}.\n\\end{align*}Then \\[\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x} = \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x} = \\frac{x^2}{y^2} = 1.\\]Setting $a = \\frac{x}{y}$, we have \\[\\frac{x}{y} + 1 + \\frac{y}{x} + \\frac{1}{\\frac{x}{y} + 1} = a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{a + 1}.\\]We can rewrite this as \\[\\frac{x}{y} + 1 + \\frac{y}{x} + \\frac{1}{\\frac{x}{y} + 1} = \\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy}{x^2 + y^2}. \\quad \\text{(1)}\\]Note that \\[\\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy}{x^2 + y^2} \\ge 2\\] by AM-GM. Therefore, \\[\\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy}{x^2 + y^2} \\ge 2.\\]Equality occurs when $x = y$. Thus, the maximum value of $\\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy}{x^2 + y^2}$ is $\\boxed{2}$. The answer is: 2",
